#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""veldo: the single front door for work, fleet, status, watch, account, and the run inbox.

This executable is a THIN dispatcher. It parses one subcommand, then routes to an
EXISTING module and calls it. It reimplements nothing: not the loop, not the
launcher, not the governor, not the reader, not the registry. A review can diff
this file and see only argument routing plus wiring.

  routing table (subcommand -> module, all pre-built in earlier work):
    veldo status            -> .veldo/runstatus.py   (the Run Lens read model)
    veldo watch             -> .veldo/runstatus.py   (the compact terminal render)
    veldo account add|list  -> .veldo/accounts.py    (the account registry, W2)
    veldo answer|steer|abort-> .veldo/runcmd.py      (the run inbox front door, R6)
    veldo work  [--account] -> .veldo/work.py over a .veldo/dispatch.py Dispatcher (W1)
    veldo fleet N [--account] -> .veldo/fleet.py launcher + W2 spawner + W3 governor
    veldo supervisor install|status|uninstall -> .veldo/supervisor.py (the opt-in,
                            off-by-default external resume timer, W7)
    veldo mirror [--dry-run] -> .veldo/tracker_mirror_runner.py (the opt-in,
                            off-by-default live mirror runner, PLAN-0010 W5; a
                            repo-only module, so a pack that did not lay it fails
                            loud and honestly rather than import a missing file)
    veldo channel status|qualify|activate|stop|delegate -> .veldo/control_channel_activation.py
                            (the owner's Telegram edge commands, signed by his enrolled
                            key and applied by the running authority service, VELDO-0138;
                            delegate renews his standing answer delegation, VELDO-0140)
    veldo factory setup      -> .veldo/control_factory_setup.py (the owner's one setup of a real
                            factory on this host from the existing pieces and his own signed
                            commands, VELDO-0139; a re-run with the same arguments completes
                            it and adds the API behind Tailscale Serve, VELDO-0171)
    veldo factory passkey    -> the same module (lists the API's pending passkey registrations, and
                            signs the one the owner names by its fingerprint, VELDO-0171)
    veldo space sign --as P  -> .veldo/control_space.py (signs one space command as a host
                            principal, space-observer or space-runner, with that principal's
                            own key from the installation folder; never the owner's, VELDO-0326)
    veldo space record|event|replay -> .veldo/control_space_spool.py (what the git hooks run:
                            a worktree added or a branch switched with plain git, a change of the
                            trunk or a landing ref; recorded through the authority as
                            space-observer, or left in the spool when it is not reachable, VELDO-0311)
    veldo space run [--station S] [--label L] -- COMMAND ...
                            -> the run wrapper (VELDO-0313): admission, the run declared with its
                            station, scratch root, label and compose project name, the command in
                            its own containment group, then the census, run finished and the
                            release; it exits with the command's own status as soon as the command
                            exits, or 75 when an act mode run must wait. With no central
                            installation it says so and runs the command unchanged
    veldo space record --kind K --path P [--ticket T], then veldo space record --confirm ITEM
    veldo space event --type T [--path P ...] [--session S] [--repository R --ref N --new C]
                            -> what a host script is about to make and what it gave up
                            (VELDO-0313), signed as space-observer; an unknown kind or event type,
                            a path outside every known root and a path on the protect list are
                            refused by name, and so are the kind scratch and a path that exists
    veldo jira init          -> .veldo/tracker_jira_init.py (bootstrap a
                            company-managed Jira project into the live board -
                            detect + provision the lifecycle statuses/workflow
                            idempotently + mirror every plan and spec, WARP-0612;
                            a repo-only module, guarded like mirror)

For the delegated read and registry subcommands the remaining argv is forwarded
verbatim to that module's own CLI, so their argument surfaces stay owned by the
module (no re-declaration here). Only work and fleet have their own tiny flag
parsing, and even they add NO control logic: they assemble the real machinery and
hand it the pieces.

The no-rogue-processes boundary (feedback_no_rogue_processes, PLAN-0007 NG1):
veldo work and veldo fleet assemble the real loop and launcher, but the intelligent
build, the fresh-context review, and the worker-spawn primitive stay DELEGATED
seams that FAIL LOUD without an agent or a real in-session start wired. This CLI
spawns NOTHING detached, with one stated exception: the supervisor of a wrapped
run (veldo space run) stays after the wrapper has returned for exactly as long as
a process the wrapped command left behind is alive. It starts nothing, stops
nothing and has no timer; it sleeps on their exit. Run bare with no agent wired, veldo work and veldo fleet
fail loud, which is the honest behavior: they are the entry points an in-session
agent drives.

An unknown subcommand, bad arguments, or an unknown account each fail loud (a
nonzero exit and an honest message), never a silent no-op. Pure stdlib."""
import argparse
import importlib.util
import os
import sys
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent

# The subcommands and which module surface each routes to (documentation the
# dispatcher below keys off; the actual call sites cite the same modules).
KNOWN_SUBCOMMANDS = ("work", "fleet", "supervisor", "mirror", "jira", "status", "watch", "account",
                     "answer", "steer", "abort", "channel", "factory", "memory", "space")

_MODS = {}


def _mod(name, rel):
    """Load a sibling module by path, the codebase convention. Each loaded module
    resolves its OWN ROOT from its real __file__, so its internal sibling loads
    still point at the repo, and this stays a pure router over them."""
    if name not in _MODS:
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(name, ROOT / rel)
        m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        spec.loader.exec_module(m)
        _MODS[name] = m
    return _MODS[name]


def _runstatus():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_runstatus", ".veldo/runstatus.py")


def _accounts():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_accounts", ".veldo/accounts.py")


def _runcmd():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_runcmd", ".veldo/runcmd.py")


def _work():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_work", ".veldo/work.py")


def _dispatch():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_dispatch", ".veldo/dispatch.py")


def _fleet():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_fleet", ".veldo/fleet.py")


def _frontier():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_frontier", ".veldo/frontier.py")


def _governor():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_governor", ".veldo/governor.py")


def _supervisor():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_supervisor", ".veldo/supervisor.py")


def _mirror():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_mirror", ".veldo/tracker_mirror_runner.py")


def _jira():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_jira", ".veldo/tracker_jira_init.py")


def _channel():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_channel", ".veldo/control_channel_activation.py")


def _factory():
    return _mod("veldo_cli_factory", ".veldo/control_factory_setup.py")


def _account_error_bases():
    """The AccountError BASE classes to catch on an account failure.

    CARRY-FORWARD (W2): fleet.py double-loads accounts.py under a private module
    name (fleet.py: ACCT = _load(...)), so its AccountError family is a DISTINCT
    set of class objects from a separately-imported accounts module. The fleet
    path raises through fleet.py's OWN copy, so catching only a freshly-imported
    accounts.UnknownAccountError would NOT match it. We catch BOTH bases, so an
    unknown account fails by name whichever copy raised it."""
    bases = []
    fl_acct = getattr(_fleet(), "ACCT", None)
    for m in (_accounts(), fl_acct):
        if m is None:
            continue
        b = getattr(m, "AccountError", None)
        if b is not None and b not in bases:
            bases.append(b)
    return tuple(bases)


class GovernorController:
    """A thin FleetController adapter over the W3 per-account governor and the
    frontier. It holds NO pacing arithmetic and NO drain logic of its own:

      desired()      IS the governor's desired count over the measured event burn.
      resume_at()    IS the governor's resume computation over the same burn.
      work_remains() IS whether the frontier still has a claimable unit in scope.
      now()          IS the wall clock.

    The launcher calls only these four; this reimplements none of them. Duck-typed
    against fleet.FleetController so it needs no import-time base class."""

    def __init__(self, governor, events_path, clock, scope=None, capabilities=None,
                 repo_root=None, claims_root=None, eligibility=None):
        self._eligibility = eligibility
        self._gov = governor
        self._events_path = events_path
        self._clock = clock
        self._scope = scope
        self._caps = list(capabilities or [])
        self._repo_root = repo_root
        self._claims_root = claims_root

    def _events(self):
        # Reuse the reader that runstatus.py already ships; do not parse the stream
        # a second way.
        return _runstatus()._read_events(self._events_path)

    def now(self):
        return self._clock()

    def desired(self):
        return self._gov.desired(self._events(), self.now())

    def resume_at(self):
        return self._gov.resume_at(self._events(), self.now())

    def work_remains(self):
        units = _frontier().claimable(
            worker_caps=self._caps, scope=self._scope,
            repo_root=self._repo_root, claims_root=self._claims_root, eligibility=self._eligibility)
        return bool(units)


def _build_governor_controller(account=None, accounts_root=None, windows=None,
                               scope=None, capabilities=None, repo_root=None,
                               claims_root=None, events_path=None, now=None,
                               pool_max=None, eligibility=None):
    """Assemble the W3 AccountGovernor over the accounts this pool paces, wrapped in
    the thin GovernorController adapter. This is wiring, not new logic: the pacing
    is the governor's, the work-remaining test is the frontier's.

    A fleet MUST be paced against a real token budget. This refuses to fabricate one
    (RULE #2 spend gate, RULE #6): the session and weekly token windows are a
    required input, and with none configured it fails loud rather than invent a
    budget. That is honest: a founder who has not declared a budget should be told,
    not silently paced against a made-up number."""
    import time as _time
    gov = _governor()
    fleet = _fleet()
    if account is not None:
        names = [account]
    else:
        names = fleet.ACCT.list_accounts(root=accounts_root)
    if not names:
        raise fleet.NoAccountAvailableError(
            "no accounts registered; add one with `veldo account add <name>` before running a fleet")
    if not windows:
        raise ValueError(
            "no fleet token budget configured: declare session and weekly token windows "
            "before pacing a fleet. Refusing to fabricate a budget.")
    pacers = [gov.AccountPacer(nm, windows, max_workers=1) for nm in names]
    governor = gov.AccountGovernor(
        pacers, pool_max=(pool_max if pool_max is not None else len(names)))
    ev_path = events_path or str(ROOT / ".veldo" / "events.jsonl")
    clock = now if callable(now) else _time.time
    return GovernorController(governor, ev_path, clock, scope=scope,
                              capabilities=capabilities, repo_root=repo_root,
                              claims_root=claims_root, eligibility=eligibility)


def _entry_gate(repo_root=None, eligibility=None, trust=None):
    """VELDO-0052: the Gate every front-door entry runs under - the wired one, none in an unenrolled
    tree, or the one control_eligibility.entry_gate builds from an enrolled repository's signed
    binding under this host's trust. A named stop is raised and route() reports it."""
    return _work().EL.entry_gate(str(repo_root or ROOT), eligibility, trust)


def cmd_work(account=None, accounts_root=None, repo_root=None, claims_root=None,
             capabilities=None, scope=None, dispatcher=None, max_units=10000,
             eligibility=None, calls=None, trust=None):
    """Assemble and run the real single-worker loop: work.veldo_work over a W1
    dispatch.Dispatcher. The Dispatcher defaults wire the executor's LiveLoop build
    hooks and the fresh-context LiveReviewer with no lander, so the intelligent
    build, the review, and the land are DELEGATED seams that FAIL LOUD without an
    agent. Nothing is fabricated.

    --account resolves that account's CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR and threads it into the
    environment, so a worker driven from this session reuses that account's saved
    login. An unknown account raises an AccountError (caught by name in route()).

    Exit: a clean drain (nothing claimable) is 0; any unit that could not be built
    or reviewed (no agent wired) is a loud nonzero, never a fabricated success."""
    if account is not None:
        acct = _accounts()
        os.environ[acct.CONFIG_DIR_ENV] = acct.resolve(account, root=accounts_root)
    gate = _entry_gate(repo_root, eligibility, trust)
    if dispatcher is None:
        dispatcher = _dispatch().Dispatcher(repo_root=repo_root, eligibility=gate, calls=calls)
    result = _work().veldo_work(
        dispatcher, capabilities=capabilities, scope=scope,
        repo_root=repo_root, claims_root=claims_root, max_units=max_units, eligibility=gate)
    outcomes = result.get("outcomes", [])
    if any(not (o.get("result") or {}).get("ok") for o in outcomes):
        return 1
    return 0


def cmd_fleet(n, account=None, accounts_root=None, scope=None, capabilities=None,
              controller=None, waiter=None, start=None, windows=None,
              repo_root=None, claims_root=None, events_path=None, now=None,
              max_ticks=100000):
    """Assemble and run the elastic launcher: fleet.veldo_account_fleet with the W2
    in-session spawner, the W3 per-account governor (via GovernorController), and
    the W3 InSessionWaiter. The pool is capped at the account-pool capacity inside
    veldo_account_fleet, so one account is never run as two workers.

    AC1 (WARP-0907): the in-session resume waiter (fleet.InSessionWaiter) is the WIRED
    DEFAULT here - when no waiter is injected a living fleet that hits every account's
    budget WAITS in-session until the governor's resume time and RE-CHECKS the desired
    count before spawning (never resuming straight into the limit), and it detaches
    NOTHING. The killed-session gap is closed by the OPT-IN, off-by-default external
    supervisor (veldo supervisor), never by a default background process.

    The spawn primitive stays the fail-loud IN-SESSION reference (fleet.in_session_
    start) unless a real in-session start is injected. This NEVER spawns a detached
    or rogue process (NG1); run bare, the fleet fails loud at the first spawn, which
    is the honest behavior. An unknown --account fails fast by name through fleet's
    own accounts copy (so the class matches what the launcher would raise)."""
    fleet = _fleet()
    if account is not None:
        # Fail fast and by name on an unknown pinned account, through fleet.py's OWN
        # accounts copy (the double-load), so the raised class is the one route()'s
        # base-tuple handler is built to catch.
        fleet.ACCT.resolve(account, root=accounts_root)
    if start is None:
        start = fleet.in_session_start
    if waiter is None:
        waiter = fleet.InSessionWaiter()
    if controller is None:
        controller = _build_governor_controller(
            account=account, accounts_root=accounts_root, windows=windows,
            scope=scope, capabilities=capabilities, repo_root=repo_root,
            claims_root=claims_root, events_path=events_path, now=now,
            eligibility=_entry_gate(repo_root))
    return fleet.veldo_account_fleet(
        controller, waiter, start, account=account, accounts_root=accounts_root,
        capabilities=capabilities, scope=scope, max_workers=n, max_ticks=max_ticks)


def build_parser():
    """The single dispatcher parser: it identifies the subcommand and slurps the
    rest for the owning module. Per-subcommand argument surfaces live in the modules
    (delegated) or in _work_args / _fleet_args (work and fleet), never re-declared here."""
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="veldo",
        description="VELDO front door: dispatch work, fleet, status, watch, account, and the run inbox.")
    ap.add_argument("subcommand", nargs="?",
                    help="one of: " + ", ".join(KNOWN_SUBCOMMANDS))
    ap.add_argument("rest", nargs=argparse.REMAINDER,
                    help="arguments passed through to the subcommand")
    return ap


def _work_args(rest):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="veldo work",
                                 description="run one real single-worker loop (W1 dispatcher).")
    ap.add_argument("--account", default=None,
                    help="run under this registered account's CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR")
    return ap.parse_args(rest)


def _fleet_args(rest):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="veldo fleet",
                                 description="run the elastic launcher (W2 spawner + W3 governor).")
    ap.add_argument("n", type=int, help="max workers (capped at the account-pool capacity)")
    ap.add_argument("--account", default=None,
                    help="pin the fleet to this one registered account")
    return ap.parse_args(rest)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VELDO-0313: the run wrapper (veldo space run) and a host script's record and event verbs
# (design 3, 6.3, 6.7 and 6.9). Nothing here deletes anything, and nothing here is a new way to
# remove: what goes at a run's end is removed by Signal's removers, after the safety rules and the
# keep step (control_space_signal, control_space_remove, control_space_keep).
#
# WHO ACTS. No verb acts as the authority or as the owner, and none falls back to either. The
# wrapper writes as space-runner and the two verbs as space-observer, each through the Ledger's own
# apply, which runs the Ledger's rule for that principal first (control_space.permit). So the wrapper
# holds, declares, measures and signals only for the run the authority issued to it, it signals only
# run finished and only with that run as the subject, and the Ledger never makes an item due that
# the owner marked Keep. An owner action from either principal is refused by the Ledger by name.
# The run itself is issued by the authority's principal and never by this process: the wiring
# carries the authority's issue (`issue`), and a wiring with none means the run is not recorded.
#
# `space` is what the installation wires in when it has a space ledger: the mapping the receiver is
# given (control_launch.space_signal), with ledger and, under their own names, what Signal is given
# (probe, kinds, remover, free, keep, owner_live, landed, queued, run, proc, borrow_root), and for
# the wrapper `issue` (the authority's issue of a run: issue(label, scope)) and, when the
# installation has its own, `group` (the containment group, below). With none every verb stands
# down and says why, and the wrapper runs its command unchanged: a wrapper never refuses the
# command it rides on (design 6.1), except for the one answer admission gives a run of a scope in
# act mode that must wait.
#
# THE WRAPPER RETURNS WHEN THE COMMAND EXITS, WITH THE COMMAND'S STATUS. It waits for nothing else,
# it has no time limit of its own, and it stops and kills nothing. What the command left behind is
# not waited for by the wrapper: it is recorded, and the run's end follows its exit.
#
# WHEN THE RUN IS OVER. The command runs under a supervisor process, in the house's containment
# group. Three reads of the kernel say whether anything of the run is left, and each is made again
# whenever the question is asked; no answer is kept:
#   the supervisor   it is the child subreaper of everything the command starts, so a process that
#                    forks twice or leaves its session is still its descendant. It stays for as
#                    long as one of them, a member of the group or a labelled process lives.
#   the group        the house's own (control_containment): a transient scope of the owner's user
#                    manager, named for the run as a dispatch's is, read through its cgroup. No cap
#                    is put on it and nothing in it is ever stopped: a wrapped command is not a
#                    worker. A host that cannot make it has no group, which is a group nobody read.
#   the label        every process in the process table whose environment carries
#                    the run's space label. This is what sees work handed to a process outside the
#                    command's tree (a server that was already running). A process whose
#                    environment cannot be read may carry the label and holds the run (fail
#                    closed, design section 7), whoever it belongs to, unless the kernel itself
#                    says it cannot be the run's: it started before the run began and is no
#                    descendant and no member of the group. Only the process reader (VELDO-0344),
#                    once the owner has installed it, reads such a process (_space_holders).
# The scratch root is declared with the supervisor as its recorded process, so rule 3 of every
# judge, in any process, holds it for as long as the kernel shows that supervisor. Run finished and
# the release are sent only when all three reads find nothing, by the wrapper when the command left
# nothing, and otherwise by the supervisor's own follower when the last of them exits. Each wait is
# a kernel notification of an exit (a pidfd): there is no interval and no timer. A group nobody
# could read, or a process nobody could read, sends nothing: no census, no signal, no release, and
# the hold stays held (design 0.6).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SPACE_STATIONS = ("build", "review", "gate")
SPACE_EVENT_TYPES = ("ref_changed", "session_started", "session_ended", "released")
# What a host script records: a worktree or an agent repository it is about to make. Scratch is
# recorded only by what creates it (the wrapper for a run, the session hook for a session).
SPACE_RECORD_KINDS = ("worktree", "agent_repository")
SPACE_WAIT = 75  # the exit of a run that must wait, the answer VELDO-0220 has for a held lock
SPACE_RUN_SCRATCH = "run-scratch"  # under the first scratch root, as the receiver's
SPACE_TEMPORARY = "TMPDIR"
SPACE_EMITTER = "space-run"
SPACE_NO_INSTALLATION = "no central installation"
SPACE_NOT_REACHABLE = "the space ledger is not reachable from this process"
SPACE_OFF = "space management is off in this installation"
SPACE_NO_ISSUER = "no authority issues a run to this process"
SPACE_SIGNAL_GIVEN = ("probe", "kinds", "remover", "free", "keep", "owner_live", "landed", "queued", "run",
                      "proc", "borrow_root")
SPACE_ENDED = ("removed", "gone")
SPACE_SLICE = "veldo-runs.slice"  # never the workers' slice: a wrapped run takes no worker's place
SPACE_PROC = "/proc"  # the kernel's process table, never a wired one: what holds a run is read here
SPACE_LABEL_VARIABLE = "VELDO_SPACE_LABEL"  # control_space_docker.LABEL_VARIABLE; the suite holds them equal
PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER = 36
PF_KTHREAD = 0x00200000


def _space_names(rest, option):
    """Whether the arguments name `option`, as `--kind K` or `--kind=K`."""
    return any(word == option or word.startswith(option + "=") for word in rest)


def _space_module(name):
    return _mod("veldo_cli_" + name, ".veldo/%s.py" % name)


def _space_wired(environ=None):
    """(space, why): what this process is wired to, and when it is nothing, why. A repository with no
    central installation has no ledger (design 6.1). With one, the ledger is the authority's: until
    the authority service serves it to this process, the CLI reaches none and says so."""
    try:
        found = _space_module("installation").resolve(environ)["found"]
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - an installation that cannot be read is not one
        found = False
    return None, (SPACE_NOT_REACHABLE if found else SPACE_NO_INSTALLATION)


def _space_usable(space, why, environ):
    """(space, why) for a verb: the wiring it was given, else what this process resolves; and nothing
    for an installation whose switch is off (design 6.8: off stops every admission check)."""
    if space is None:
        space, why = _space_wired(environ) if why is None else (None, why)
    if space is not None and space["ledger"].config.get("enabled") is not True:
        return None, SPACE_OFF
    return space, why


class _SpaceAs:
    """A Ledger whose writes are one host principal's: each goes through the Ledger's own apply, so
    the Ledger's rule for that principal is run before the command. Every read is the Ledger's."""

    def __init__(self, ledger, module, principal):
        self._ledger, self._sp, self._principal = ledger, module, principal

    def __getattr__(self, name):
        return getattr(self._ledger, name)

    def _apply(self, operation, **parameters):
        import uuid
        return self._ledger.apply({
            "principal": self._principal, "operation": operation, "command_id": "space:" + uuid.uuid4().hex,
            "parameters": {name: value for name, value in parameters.items() if value is not None}})

    def declare(self, kind, locator, owner, creator, parent=None, start=None, branch=None, process=None,
                pool=None, principal=None, command_id=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.DECLARE, kind=kind, locator=locator, owner=owner, creator=creator,
                           parent=parent, start=start, branch=branch, process=process, pool=pool)

    def confirm(self, item, principal=None, command_id=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.CONFIRM, item=item)

    def measure(self, item, size, event, principal=None, command_id=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.MEASURE, item=item, bytes=size, event=event)

    def outcome(self, item, outcome, facts=None, principal=None, command_id=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.OUTCOME, item=item, outcome=outcome, facts=facts)

    def signal(self, event, subject, principal=None, command_id=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.SIGNAL, event=event, subject=subject)

    def place_hold(self, identity, needs, source, principal=None, command_id=None, scope=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.HOLD, hold=identity, needs=needs, source=source)

    def admit(self, identity, answer, reason=None, used=None, retry_asked=None, principal=None, command_id=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.ADMIT, hold=identity, answer=answer, reason=reason, used=used,
                           retry_asked=retry_asked)

    def release(self, identity, used=None, principal=None, command_id=None):
        return self._apply(self._sp.RELEASE, hold=identity, used=used)

    def event(self, kind, subject, emitter):
        return self._apply(self._sp.EVENT, type=kind, subject=subject, emitter=emitter)


def _space_runner(space):
    sp = _space_module("control_space")
    return _SpaceAs(space["ledger"], sp, sp.RUNNER)


def _space_inside(path, root):
    path, root = os.path.realpath(str(path)), os.path.realpath(str(root))
    return path == root or path.startswith(root.rstrip(os.sep) + os.sep)


def _space_scope(ledger, sp, where):
    """The scope a folder belongs to (design 6.7): the project or plain repository it is inside (its
    repository or its worktree root), else no project."""
    for project in ledger.projects:
        if _space_inside(where, project["repository"]) or _space_inside(where, project["worktree_root"]):
            return {"type": "project", "id": project["project"]}
    for plain in ledger.config.get("plain_repositories") or ():
        if _space_inside(where, plain["repository"]) or _space_inside(where, plain["worktree_root"]):
            return {"type": "plain_repository", "id": sp.canonical_path(plain["repository"])}
    return {"type": "no_project"}


def _space_path_refusal(ledger, sp, path, kind="scratch"):
    """The name the Ledger refuses a path by, read with the Ledger's own functions against its own
    protect list and known roots, as written and where it leads; None for a path it would take.
    The order is declare's: the protect list, the known roots, a link."""
    try:
        canonical = sp.canonical_locator(kind, {"path": path})
    except sp.Refused as refusal:
        return refusal.code
    resolved = {"path": sp.resolved_path(canonical["path"])}
    entry = (sp.protected_by(kind, canonical, ledger.protect)
             or sp.protected_by(kind, resolved, ledger.protect, sp.resolved_path))
    if entry is not None:
        return "space_protected:" + entry
    if not sp.inside_roots(kind, resolved, ledger.resolved_roots, ledger.engines):
        return "space_outside_roots"
    if resolved != canonical:
        return "space_locator_link"
    return None


def _space_worktree(ledger, where):
    """(identity, record) of the live worktree or agent repository item the folder is in, the
    innermost one, or None."""
    found = None
    for identity, record in sorted(ledger.items().items()):
        path = (record.get("locator") or {}).get("path")
        if (record.get("kind") in ("worktree", "agent_repository") and record.get("state") not in SPACE_ENDED
                and path and _space_inside(where, path)
                and (found is None or len(path) > len(found[1]["locator"]["path"]))):
            found = (identity, record)
    return found


def _space_needs(ledger, scope, station, places):
    """({pool: bytes}, {pool: source}) of a run of `station`: for each filesystem pool one of `places`
    is on, what the project's space file declares for the station, else the pool's default need from
    the installation."""
    devices = {ledger.device(os.path.realpath(str(place))) for place in places}
    declared = {}
    for project in ledger.projects:
        if scope.get("type") == "project" and project.get("project") == scope.get("id"):
            declared = ((project.get("needs") or {}).get(station) or {})
    needs, source = {}, {}
    for name, pool in sorted((ledger.config.get("pools") or {}).items()):
        record = ledger.pool(name)
        if not record or record.get("type") != "filesystem" or record.get("device") not in devices:
            continue
        if name in declared:
            needs[name], source[name] = declared[name], "declared"
        else:
            needs[name], source[name] = pool["needs"][station], "default"
    return needs, source


def _space_numbers(reason):
    return "pool %s: need %d, free %d, held by other runs %d, floor %d (bytes)" % (
        reason.get("pool"), reason.get("need"), reason.get("free"), reason.get("held"), reason.get("floor"))


def _space_make(path):
    """Make the run's scratch folder, and the folder it is in."""
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    os.mkdir(path, 0o700)


def _space_begin(space, station, label, where, start=None):
    """Before the command starts: the run the authority issues to space-runner, its hold and the
    answer of admission, and, for a run that starts, its scratch root, declared before it is made,
    and the environment that carries its temporary folder, its space label and its compose project
    name. `start()`, when given, starts the run's supervisor once admission has let the run start,
    and returns its process, {pid, started}: the scratch root is declared with it, so the item is
    held for as long as the kernel shows that process. Raises when the run could not be issued;
    everything after that is noted and goes on. What it returns is plain data."""
    import hashlib
    sp, ledger = _space_module("control_space"), space["ledger"]
    runner = _space_runner(space)
    scope = _space_scope(ledger, sp, where)
    # The run's identity is the authority's to issue, and the authority's principal writes it: this
    # process never calls the Ledger's issue. A wiring with no issuer records no run.
    issuer = space.get("issue")
    if not callable(issuer):
        raise RuntimeError(SPACE_NO_ISSUER)
    issued = issuer(label, scope)
    if not isinstance(issued, dict) or not issued.get("ok") or ledger.issued_to(issued.get("run")) != sp.RUNNER:
        raise RuntimeError("the run was not issued: %s" % ((issued or {}).get("reason")
                                                           if isinstance(issued, dict) else "no answer",))
    run = issued["run"]
    out = {"run": run, "scope": scope, "station": station, "hold": None, "answer": None,
           "reason": None, "worktree": None, "owner": None, "scratch": None, "items": {}, "environment": {},
           "notes": [], "supervisor": None, "group": None, "status": None, "unread": False}
    roots = list((ledger.config.get("scratch_roots") or {}).get("roots") or ())
    try:
        needs, source = _space_needs(ledger, scope, station, [where] + roots[:1])
        if needs:
            placed = runner.place_hold(run, needs, source)
            if not placed.get("ok"):
                raise RuntimeError("hold refused: %s" % (placed.get("reason"),))
            out["hold"] = run
            answer = _space_module("control_space_admission").admit(runner, run, space["free"])
            if not answer.get("ok"):
                raise RuntimeError("admit refused: %s" % (answer.get("reason"),))
            out["answer"], out["reason"] = answer["answer"], answer["reason"]
        else:
            out["notes"].append("no pool of the installation holds this folder: admission was not asked")
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - admission that could not be asked never stops the command
        out["notes"].append("admission was not judged: %s" % (str(error)[:300],))
    if out["answer"] == "waiting":
        return out
    if start is not None:
        try:
            out["supervisor"] = start()
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - a run with no supervisor is a run nobody can read
            out["notes"].append("the run's supervisor was not started: %s" % (str(error)[:300],))
    try:
        out["environment"].update(_space_module("control_space_docker").given(run)["environment"])
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
        out["notes"].append("the run's label was not given: %s" % (str(error)[:300],))
    held = _space_worktree(ledger, where)
    if held is not None:
        out["worktree"] = held[1]["locator"]["path"]
    if scope["type"] == "no_project":
        out["notes"].append("the folder is inside no project and no plain repository: no folder is recorded")
        return out
    owner = {"scope": dict(scope), "run": {"id": run, "station": station}}
    if held is not None and (held[1].get("owner") or {}).get("scope") == scope and held[1]["owner"].get("ticket"):
        owner["ticket"] = held[1]["owner"]["ticket"]
    out["owner"] = owner
    try:
        if not roots:
            raise RuntimeError("the installation names no scratch root")
        path = os.path.join(sp.canonical_path(str(roots[0])), SPACE_RUN_SCRATCH,
                            hashlib.sha256(run.encode()).hexdigest()[:32])
        if os.path.lexists(path):
            # never taken over: whatever is in it was not made by this run
            raise RuntimeError("the scratch folder %s is there and no item records it" % path)
        made = runner.declare("scratch", {"path": path}, owner, "wrapper", process=out["supervisor"])
        if not made.get("ok"):
            raise RuntimeError("declare refused: %s" % (made.get("reason"),))
        out["items"][path] = made["item"]
        try:
            _space_make(path)
        except OSError as error:
            # The item stays declared, with nothing at its locator: gone is not a command of
            # space-runner, and reconcile ends an item whose thing was never made (design 8.8).
            raise RuntimeError("the scratch folder could not be made (%s): its item %s stays declared, for "
                               "reconcile" % (type(error).__name__, made["item"]))
        confirmed = runner.confirm(made["item"])
        if not confirmed.get("ok"):
            raise RuntimeError("confirm refused: %s" % (confirmed.get("reason"),))
        out["scratch"] = path
        out["environment"][SPACE_TEMPORARY] = path
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - a run with no recorded scratch keeps its own temporary folder
        out["notes"].append("no scratch root was given: %s" % (str(error)[:300],))
    return out


def _space_code(raw):
    code = os.waitstatus_to_exitcode(raw)
    return 128 - code if code < 0 else code


def _space_handlers(handlers):
    """Install signal handlers, where this thread may; returns what to give back to _space_restore."""
    import signal
    before = {}
    for number, handler in handlers.items():
        try:
            before[number] = signal.signal(number, handler)
        except (ValueError, OSError):  # not the main thread: the signals stay as they are
            pass
    return before


def _space_restore(before):
    import signal
    for number, handler in before.items():
        try:
            signal.signal(number, handler)
        except (ValueError, OSError, TypeError):
            pass


def _space_process(pid):
    """The kernel's record of one live process: {pid, started, parent, flags}, its start time in
    clock ticks since boot (field 22 of stat). None when the kernel does not show it, or shows what
    is left of one that has ended."""
    try:
        with open("%s/%d/stat" % (SPACE_PROC, int(pid)), "rb") as stat:
            fields = stat.read().rsplit(b")", 1)[1].split()
        if fields[0] in (b"Z", b"X"):
            return None
        return {"pid": int(pid), "started": int(fields[19]), "parent": int(fields[1]), "flags": int(fields[6])}
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def _space_identity(pid):
    """What one process is at this moment, as the kernel shows it: its start time, the two ends of
    its environment (fields 22, 50 and 51 of stat) and the device and inode of its program. Raises
    what the kernel raises, and ValueError for a stat that is not in the format."""
    with open("%s/%d/stat" % (SPACE_PROC, int(pid)), "rb") as stat:
        fields = stat.read().rsplit(b")", 1)[1].split()
    if len(fields) < 49:
        raise ValueError("stat has no environment bounds")
    program = os.stat("%s/%d/exe" % (SPACE_PROC, int(pid)))
    return (int(fields[19]), int(fields[47]), int(fields[48]), program.st_dev, program.st_ino)


def _space_environment(pid):
    """The entries of one process's environment, from a whole read of it, or None when it was not
    read whole. The kernel reads environ from the memory the process had when the entry was opened:
    a process that executes a program meanwhile has other memory by then, and the read returns
    nothing, or a part. Bytes that end early with no label among them are not an environment
    without the label (the review of VELDO-0344, finding 1). So what the process is, is taken
    before the read and after it, and the read is whole only when the two agree, at least one byte
    was returned, and exactly as many as the bounds are apart; an environment of no bytes is never
    whole, since that is what the kernel returns for a process that is executing. A read that is
    not whole is made once more. Raises what the kernel raises: the caller tells a process that has
    ended from one it may not read."""
    for _ in range(2):
        try:
            before = _space_identity(pid)
            with open("%s/%d/environ" % (SPACE_PROC, int(pid)), "rb") as environment:
                read = environment.read()
            after = _space_identity(pid)
        except (ValueError, IndexError):
            continue
        if before == after and read and len(read) == before[2] - before[1]:
            return read.split(b"\0")
    return None


class _SpaceScope:
    """The house's containment group for a wrapped run (control_containment, the provider the
    receiver uses for a dispatch): a transient scope of the owner's user manager, named for the run
    by the house's own rule, in a slice of its own. It carries no cap and is never stopped: nothing
    of a wrapped run is ever ended by Veldo. It is read through its cgroup, as the receiver reads a
    dispatch's."""

    def __init__(self, environ=None):
        import shutil
        self.house = _space_module("control_containment")
        self.environment = self.house.tool_environment(environ)
        self.tool = shutil.which("systemd-run", path=self.environment.get("PATH", os.defpath))

    def words(self, run):
        """The command words that start what follows them in the run's own scope, or None on a host
        that cannot make one."""
        if (not self.tool or not sys.platform.startswith("linux") or not self.environment.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR")
                or not (self.house.CGROUP / "cgroup.controllers").is_file()):
            return None
        return [self.tool, "--user", "--scope", "--quiet", "--collect", "--unit=" + self.house.unit_name(run),
                "--slice=" + SPACE_SLICE, "--description=Veldo wrapped run " + str(run), "--"]

    def read(self, run, group):
        """{read, populated, pids} of the run's group (_space_scope_read), by the house's own name
        for the run's scope and under the house's own cgroup root."""
        return _space_scope_read(str(self.house.CGROUP), self.house.unit_name(run), group)


def _space_scope_read(root, unit, group):
    """{read, populated, pids}: whether a run's group could be read, whether the kernel shows a
    process in it (its cgroup.events, which counts every group beneath it too), and the processes
    it lists. A run with no group record, a record that is not the scope `unit`, and a group whose
    files cannot be read are not read. A group whose folder is gone, or goes while it is read, is
    empty: the kernel removes it only then."""
    unread = {"read": False, "populated": None, "pids": []}
    cgroup = (group or {}).get("cgroup") if isinstance(group, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(cgroup, str) or not unit or cgroup.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] != unit:
        return unread
    path = os.path.join(str(root), cgroup.lstrip("/"))
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(path, "cgroup.events")) as events:
            populated = "populated 1" in events.read().splitlines()
        with open(os.path.join(path, "cgroup.procs")) as members:
            pids = [int(word) for word in members.read().split()]
    except (OSError, ValueError) as error:
        import errno
        # A group the kernel has removed, or is removing as it is read (ENODEV), is empty: it removes
        # a group only then. Anything else that cannot be read is not read.
        gone = getattr(error, "errno", None) == errno.ENODEV or not os.path.lexists(path)
        return {"read": True, "populated": False, "pids": []} if gone else unread
    return {"read": True, "populated": populated, "pids": pids}


def _space_group(space, environ=None):
    """The containment group provider: the installation's own when it wires one, else the house's."""
    wired = (space or {}).get("group")
    return wired if wired is not None else _SpaceScope(environ)


def _space_reader(space):
    """The process reader's own read (the probe's reader_read, VELDO-0344), as a callable, from the
    probe the installation wires in, else from the house's. None when there is none to ask."""
    try:
        probe = (space or {}).get("probe") or _space_module("control_space_probe")
        return probe.reader_read
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - no probe is no reader: every unread process holds
        return None


def _space_holders(run, supervisor, members, since=None, reader=None):
    """What the kernel shows of the run now: [{pid, started, how}], read in one pass over the
    process table. `how` is supervisor (the run's supervisor itself, which stays while anything of
    the run lives), descendant (a process that descends from it), group (a member of the run's
    containment group), label (a process whose environment carries the run's space label, read here
    or, for a process this account may not read, by the process reader) or unread
    (a process whose environment cannot be read, or was not read whole (_space_environment), and
    that may be the run's, below). `supervisor` is
    the process the descendants are counted from, {pid, started}; in the supervisor itself it is
    this process, which is then not listed. Nothing here signals a process.

    UNREAD HOLDS (fail closed, design section 7; the owner's decision, Telegram 41204 and 41205). A
    process whose environment cannot be read may carry the run's label, so it holds the run for as
    long as it lives, whoever it belongs to and whatever started it. One thing takes it out, and it
    is what the kernel says with no environment read: the process started before `since`, the start
    of the run's supervisor, and it is no descendant of the supervisor and no member of the group.
    Such a process got its environment before the run had a label. A kernel thread has no
    environment at all and is not a process in this sense (PF_KTHREAD, in the stat file every user
    may read). With no `since` nothing is taken out.

    THE READER'S LABEL. `reader`, when given, is the process reader's read (VELDO-0344). It is asked
    only when a process is unread. The reader reads, for every task, the value of the run label
    variable in the task's environment, and the probe gives it as the block's label: a list of the
    values read, empty when the environment was read and carries none, and None when it was not
    read. The label decides, and only the label: with the reader's state used, a block that is whole,
    has the start time the kernel shows here and has a label that was read, the process is a holder
    by its label when one of the values is this run, and no holder when none is (it carries no run,
    or another one; rule 4 judges it by the files it holds). A block with no label read rules nothing
    out, whole or not: the reader saw the process, not what it carries. A reader that is absent or
    refused, a process with no block, a block that is not whole and one with another start time
    change nothing either: the process holds."""
    label = (SPACE_LABEL_VARIABLE + "=" + str(run)).encode()
    me = os.getpid()
    table = {}
    try:
        names = os.listdir(SPACE_PROC)
    except OSError:
        return [{"pid": None, "started": None, "how": "unread"}]
    for name in names:
        if name.isdigit():
            seen = _space_process(int(name))
            if seen is not None:
                table[seen["pid"]] = seen
    root = (supervisor or {}).get("pid")
    if root is not None and root != me and (table.get(root) or {}).get("started") != (supervisor or {}).get("started"):
        root = None  # that process has ended: nothing descends from it any more
    found = {}

    def add(pid, how):
        if pid != me and pid in table and pid not in found:
            found[pid] = {"pid": pid, "started": table[pid]["started"], "how": how}
    if root is not None:
        add(root, "supervisor")
        for pid in sorted(table):
            at, steps = pid, 0
            while at in table and at != root and steps < 4096:
                at, steps = table[at]["parent"], steps + 1
            if at == root and pid != root:
                add(pid, "descendant")
    for pid in members or ():
        add(pid, "group")
    unread = []
    for pid in sorted(table):
        if pid == me or table[pid]["flags"] & PF_KTHREAD:
            continue
        try:
            carried = _space_environment(pid)
        except (FileNotFoundError, ProcessLookupError):
            continue  # it ended while it was read
        except OSError:
            carried = None
        if carried is None:  # refused, or not read whole: nobody read what it carries
            if _space_process(pid) is not None:
                unread.append(pid)
        elif label in carried:
            add(pid, "label")
    # The one thing that takes an unread process out: it started before the run began, and it is no
    # descendant and no member of the group (those are in `found` already, under their own names).
    unread = [pid for pid in unread if pid not in found and (since is None or table[pid]["started"] >= since)]
    blocks = None
    if unread and reader is not None:
        try:
            said = reader()
            if isinstance(said, dict) and said.get("state") == "used" and isinstance(said.get("blocks"), dict):
                blocks = said["blocks"]
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a reader that could not be asked read nothing
            blocks = None
    wanted = str(run).encode()
    for pid in unread:
        block, carried = (blocks or {}).get(pid), None
        if isinstance(block, dict) and block.get("whole") is True and block.get("start") == table[pid]["started"]:
            carried = block.get("label")
        if not isinstance(carried, (list, tuple)):
            add(pid, "unread")  # nobody read what it carries: it may be the run's
        elif wanted in carried:
            add(pid, "label")  # the reader read this run's label in its environment
    return [found[pid] for pid in sorted(found)]


def _space_judged(space, given, environ=None):
    """{group, holders}: what holds the run now, read from the kernel at this call. group is unread,
    populated or empty."""
    read = _space_group(space, environ).read(given["run"], given.get("group"))
    if not isinstance(read, dict) or read.get("read") is not True:
        return {"group": "unread", "holders": []}
    supervisor = given.get("supervisor")
    holders = _space_holders(given["run"], supervisor, read.get("pids"), since=(supervisor or {}).get("started"),
                             reader=_space_reader(space))
    return {"group": "populated" if read.get("populated") or any(one["how"] == "group" for one in holders)
            else "empty", "holders": holders}


def _space_wait(read, reap=None, asleep=None):
    """Sleep until nothing that can be waited for holds the run, and return the last read. Each
    sleep is one poll with no time limit on a pidfd of every holder: the kernel wakes it when one
    of them exits, and the table is then read again. A process whose environment cannot be read is
    waited for like any other: its exit needs no reading. Nothing is signalled. What nobody can
    wait for (a group nobody read, a table nobody could list) ends the wait at once: the caller
    sends nothing for it. `asleep`, when given, is called once, when the first sleep is about to
    begin: every holder's pidfd is open and registered then."""
    import select
    while True:
        if reap is not None:
            reap()
        seen = read()
        waited = [one for one in seen["holders"] if one["pid"] is not None]
        if seen["group"] == "unread" or not waited:
            return seen
        poller, descriptors = select.poll(), []
        try:
            for one in waited:
                try:
                    descriptor = os.pidfd_open(one["pid"])
                except OSError:
                    break  # it ended since the read: read again
                descriptors.append(descriptor)
                now = _space_process(one["pid"])
                if now is None or now["started"] != one["started"]:
                    break
                poller.register(descriptor, select.POLLIN)
            else:
                if asleep is not None:
                    asleep, begun = None, asleep
                    begun()
                poller.poll()
        finally:
            for descriptor in descriptors:
                os.close(descriptor)


def _space_let_go():
    """Point descriptors 0, 1 and 2 of this process at /dev/null: what a supervisor that stays does
    first, so that it holds no end of a pipe its caller reads. On a host with no /dev/null they stay
    as they are: a descriptor is never closed here, since the next file opened would take its
    number."""
    for stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):
        try:
            stream.flush()
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - what could not be written is not kept for later
            pass
    try:
        null = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_RDWR)
    except OSError:
        return
    try:
        for number in (0, 1, 2):
            os.dup2(null, number)
    finally:
        if null > 2:
            os.close(null)


def space_contained(rest):
    """veldo space contained --report FD -- COMMAND ...: the first thing that runs inside a run's
    scope. It writes the cgroup the kernel shows for itself to FD, closes it and becomes the
    command, in the same process."""
    import json
    if len(rest) < 4 or rest[0] != "--report" or rest[2] != "--" or not rest[1].isdigit():
        sys.stderr.write("veldo space contained: the wrapper's own verb\n")
        return 2
    descriptor, command = int(rest[1]), rest[3:]
    try:
        with open("/proc/self/cgroup") as shown:
            cgroup = next((line[3:].strip() for line in shown if line.startswith("0::")), None)
        os.write(descriptor, (json.dumps({"cgroup": cgroup}) + "\n").encode())
        os.close(descriptor)
    except OSError:
        pass
    try:
        os.execvp(command[0], command)
    except OSError as error:
        sys.stderr.write("veldo space run: %s: %s\n" % (command[0], error.strerror or error))
        return 127 if isinstance(error, FileNotFoundError) else 126


def space_supervise(rest):
    """veldo space supervise --report FD --control FD -- COMMAND ...: what the wrapper starts in
    place of the command. It makes itself the child subreaper and waits for one line on the control
    descriptor: the run, what to add to the command's environment, and the words that put the
    command in the run's scope. It starts the command with its own input, output and folder, and
    writes one JSON line each to the report descriptor: group (the scope the command is in, or
    none), and, when the command exits, status with the processes of the run the kernel still
    shows. With none it exits with the command's status. Otherwise it stays, asleep on their exit,
    and when the last has gone the run's end is made by a process that follows this one, with the
    wiring that process resolves for itself. It signals nothing but the command itself, with the
    stop it was sent, and only while the command runs.

    BEFORE IT STAYS it lets go of the caller's input and output: descriptors 0, 1 and 2 are pointed
    at /dev/null before the status line is written, and the process that follows inherits that. So
    a caller that reads the wrapper's output to its end gets the end when the wrapper returns, and
    not when what the command left exits. Nothing changes for the command, which has ended, or for
    what it left, which holds its own descriptors. Then it writes one more line, staying: true from
    inside the wait, when the pidfd of every holder is open and its first sleep is about to begin,
    and false when it did not sleep at all."""
    import json
    import signal
    import subprocess
    if (len(rest) < 6 or rest[0] != "--report" or rest[2] != "--control" or rest[4] != "--"
            or not rest[1].isdigit() or not rest[3].isdigit()):
        sys.stderr.write("veldo space supervise: the wrapper's own verb\n")
        return 2
    descriptor, command = int(rest[1]), rest[5:]

    def tell(**what):
        try:
            os.write(descriptor, (json.dumps(what, sort_keys=True) + "\n").encode())
        except OSError:
            pass
    try:
        import ctypes
        subreaper = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True).prctl(PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER, 1, 0, 0, 0) == 0
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a host with no subreaper: what the command leaves is not this process's
        subreaper = False
    started = {}

    def forward(number, frame):
        if started.get("pid"):
            try:
                os.kill(started["pid"], number)
            except OSError:
                pass
    # A stop from the terminal reaches the command by itself; one sent to this process is passed on
    # to the command while it runs, and to nothing after it.
    _space_handlers({signal.SIGINT: lambda number, frame: None, signal.SIGQUIT: lambda number, frame: None,
                     signal.SIGTERM: forward, signal.SIGHUP: forward})
    try:
        with os.fdopen(int(rest[3]), "rb") as control:
            said = json.loads(control.readline().decode() or "null")
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        said = None
    if not isinstance(said, dict):
        return 1  # the wrapper went before it said what to run: nothing is started
    run, given = said.get("run"), said.get("given") if isinstance(said.get("given"), dict) else {}
    environment = dict(os.environ, **{str(name): str(value) for name, value in (said.get("environment") or {}).items()})
    scope, group, child = said.get("scope"), None, None
    try:
        if isinstance(scope, list) and scope:
            reading, writing = os.pipe()
            try:
                child = subprocess.Popen(
                    [str(word) for word in scope] + [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "space", "contained",
                                                     "--report", str(writing), "--"] + command,
                    env=dict(environment, **(said.get("scope_environment") or {})), pass_fds=(writing,))
            finally:
                os.close(writing)
            with os.fdopen(reading, "rb") as lines:
                try:
                    shown = json.loads(lines.readline().decode() or "null")
                except ValueError:
                    shown = None
            if isinstance(shown, dict) and isinstance(shown.get("cgroup"), str):
                group = {"unit": said.get("unit"), "cgroup": shown["cgroup"]}
            else:
                # The scope was not made, so the command was not started in it. It still runs.
                child.wait()
                child = None
        if child is None:
            child = subprocess.Popen(command, env=environment, close_fds=True)
    except OSError as error:
        sys.stderr.write("veldo space run: %s: %s\n" % (command[0], error.strerror or error))
        status = 127 if isinstance(error, FileNotFoundError) else 126
        tell(group=None)
        tell(status=status, holders=[], read=True, failed=type(error).__name__)
        return status
    tell(group=group, subreaper=subreaper)
    started["pid"], status = child.pid, None
    while status is None:
        try:
            pid, raw = os.waitpid(-1, 0)
        except ChildProcessError:
            status = 1
            break
        if pid == child.pid:
            status = child.returncode = _space_code(raw)
    started["pid"] = None
    mine = _space_process(os.getpid()) or {}
    me = {"pid": os.getpid(), "started": mine.get("started")}

    def reap():
        """Collect every child that has ended. True when the kernel says this process has no child
        left at all, which is one answer of the kernel and not a pass over the table."""
        try:
            while os.waitpid(-1, os.WNOHANG)[0]:
                pass
        except ChildProcessError:
            return True
        return False

    def read():
        # With no scope and no subreaper, what the command left is in nobody's reach: not read.
        shown = (_space_scope_read(said.get("cgroup_root"), said.get("unit"), group) if group is not None
                 else {"read": subreaper, "populated": False, "pids": []})
        if shown.get("read") is not True:
            return {"group": "unread", "holders": []}
        while True:
            childless = reap()
            holders = _space_holders(run, me, shown.get("pids"), since=me["started"], reader=_space_reader(None))
            if childless or any(one["how"] == "descendant" for one in holders):
                return {"group": "populated" if shown.get("populated") else "empty", "holders": holders}
            # A child is alive that this pass over the table did not show: it was started while the
            # table was read. The table is read again; the child is in it now.
    seen = read()
    if seen["holders"]:
        _space_let_go()
    tell(status=status, holders=seen["holders"], read=seen["group"] != "unread")
    if not seen["holders"]:
        return status
    # Something of the run is left. This process is the one the run's scratch root records, so it
    # stays for as long as any of it lives, asleep on their exit.
    _space_wait(read, reap, asleep=lambda: tell(staying=True))
    tell(staying=False)  # for a wrapper that has heard nothing yet: there was nothing to sleep on
    space, why = _space_usable(None, None, None)
    if space is None:
        tell(end=why)
        return status  # nobody serves the ledger to this process: the items and the hold stay as they are
    # The run's end is made by a process that follows this one: the scratch root records this
    # process, and rule 3 holds the item while the kernel shows it.
    reading, writing = os.pipe()
    if os.fork():
        return status
    os.close(writing)
    os.read(reading, 1)  # returns when the process above has ended
    _space_finish(space, dict(given, run=run, status=status, group=group, supervisor=me))
    os._exit(0)


class _SpaceSupervisor:
    """The wrapper's side of the run's supervisor: it is started before the scratch root is
    declared, so the item can record it, and it starts the command only when it is told to."""

    def __init__(self, command, environ, where):
        self.command, self.environ, self.where = list(command), environ, where
        self.child, self.lines, self.control, self.process, self.staying = None, None, None, None, False

    def start(self):
        """Start the supervisor, which then waits for go. Returns its process, {pid, started}."""
        import subprocess
        reading, writing = os.pipe()
        heard, told = os.pipe()
        try:
            self.child = subprocess.Popen(
                [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "space", "supervise", "--report", str(writing),
                 "--control", str(heard), "--"] + self.command,
                env=self.environ, cwd=self.where, pass_fds=(writing, heard))
        except BaseException:
            os.close(reading)
            os.close(told)
            raise
        finally:
            os.close(writing)
            os.close(heard)
        self.lines, self.control = os.fdopen(reading, "rb"), told
        seen = _space_process(self.child.pid)
        self.process = {"pid": self.child.pid, "started": seen["started"]} if seen else None
        return self.process

    def go(self, given, scope):
        """Tell the supervisor what to run it as: the run, its environment, and its scope's words."""
        import json
        house = scope if isinstance(scope, _SpaceScope) else None
        words = None
        try:
            words = scope.words(given["run"])
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a group that cannot be made is a group nobody reads
            pass
        line = {"run": given["run"], "environment": given["environment"], "scope": words, "given": given,
                "unit": house.house.unit_name(given["run"]) if house is not None else None,
                "cgroup_root": str(house.house.CGROUP) if house is not None else None,
                "scope_environment": ({"XDG_RUNTIME_DIR": house.environment["XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"]}
                                      if house is not None and words else {})}
        try:
            os.write(self.control, (json.dumps(line, sort_keys=True) + "\n").encode())
        finally:
            os.close(self.control)
            self.control = None

    def result(self):
        """(status, holders, group) as the supervisor said them when the command exited: the
        command's own exit status, the processes of the run the kernel still showed, and the scope
        the command ran in. This returns at the command's exit and waits for nothing the command
        left. With no word of the command the supervisor's own end is the status and holders is
        None. When something is left, one more line of the supervisor is read, or the end of its
        lines: self.staying is True when the supervisor said it is asleep on the exit of what is
        left, and False when it said otherwise or ended without a word."""
        import json
        import signal
        child = self.child
        forward = lambda number, frame: child.poll() is None and child.send_signal(number)  # noqa: E731
        before = _space_handlers({signal.SIGINT: lambda number, frame: None, signal.SIGTERM: forward,
                                  signal.SIGHUP: forward})
        try:
            status, holders, group = None, None, None
            with self.lines as lines:
                for line in lines:
                    try:
                        said = json.loads(line.decode())
                    except ValueError:
                        continue
                    if not isinstance(said, dict):
                        continue
                    if "group" in said:
                        group = said["group"] if isinstance(said["group"], dict) else None
                    if isinstance(said.get("status"), int):
                        status = said["status"]
                        holders = said["holders"] if isinstance(said.get("holders"), list) else None
                        if holders == []:
                            child.wait()  # it has nothing to stay for, and the scratch root records it
                        if said.get("read") is not True:
                            holders = None  # the supervisor could not read what the command left
                        if not holders:
                            break
                    if status is not None and "staying" in said:
                        self.staying = said["staying"] is True
                        break
            if status is None:
                code = child.wait()
                status = 128 - code if code < 0 else code
            return status, holders, group
        finally:
            _space_restore(before)

    def drop(self):
        """A supervisor that will not be told to go: it ends without starting anything."""
        if self.control is not None:
            os.close(self.control)
            self.control = None
        if self.lines is not None:
            self.lines.close()
        if self.child is not None:
            self.child.wait()


def _space_plain(command, environ, where):
    """The command, unchanged: its own arguments, environment, folder, input and output, and its own
    exit status."""
    import subprocess
    try:
        code = subprocess.call(list(command), env=environ, cwd=where)
    except OSError as error:
        sys.stderr.write("veldo space run: %s: %s\n" % (command[0], error.strerror or error))
        return 127 if isinstance(error, FileNotFoundError) else 126
    return 128 - code if code < 0 else code


def _space_docker(space, runner, given):
    """The Docker part of the census (design 6.3, step 3): on every engine the installation names,
    each container, network and volume that carries the run's label or the run's compose project
    name is declared as space-runner, owned by the run, with the identity the engine showed, and
    confirmed. Whose an object is is the adapter's own rule (control_space_docker.owner_of), asked
    for this run alone: an object with another run's label, or with none, gets nothing here. An
    object the protect list covers, a container that mounts what is protected, and one shown with
    no identity get no item. An engine that cannot be read is named and its objects are left.
    Returns {engines: {engine: read or what was said}, declared: {item: locator}, held, refused}."""
    import json
    docker, ledger, run = _space_module("control_space_docker"), space["ledger"], given["run"]
    answer = {"engines": {}, "declared": {}, "held": [], "refused": {}}
    if not given.get("owner"):
        return answer  # a run with no project owns no Docker object
    client = space.get("run") or docker.Adapter(ledger).run
    # An engine with no image pool of its own counts a run's objects against the pool the run holds.
    try:
        pool = next(iter(sorted((ledger.hold(given["hold"]) or {}).get("needs") or {})), None)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        pool = None
    pool = pool or next(iter(sorted(ledger.config.get("pools") or {})), None)
    protect = list(ledger.protect or ())
    for engine in ledger.engines:
        shown, said = {}, None
        for kind in docker.KINDS:
            code, out = client(["docker", "--context", str(engine)] + list(docker.LISTED[kind]))
            names = str(out).split() if code == 0 else None
            if names:
                code, out = client(["docker", "--context", str(engine), kind, "inspect"] + names)
            try:
                records = json.loads(out) if names and code == 0 else []
            except ValueError:
                records = None
            if code != 0 or not isinstance(records, list) or not all(isinstance(one, dict) for one in records):
                said = "%s: %s" % (kind, str(out)[:200] or "the client gave no answer")
                break
            shown[kind] = records
        if said is not None:
            answer["engines"][engine] = said  # not read: nothing of this engine is recorded
            continue
        answer["engines"][engine] = "read"
        for kind in docker.KINDS:
            for record in shown[kind]:
                locator = docker.locator_of(engine, kind, record)
                if locator is None or (kind == "network" and locator["name"] in docker.ENGINE_NETWORKS):
                    continue
                label, owner, rule = docker.owner_of(locator, {run: given["owner"]})
                if owner is None:
                    continue
                name = "%s:%s:%s" % (engine, kind, locator["name"])
                if docker.IDENTITY[kind][0] not in locator:
                    answer["refused"][name] = "no_identity"
                    continue
                entry = docker.covered(engine, kind, [locator["name"]], docker.labels_of(kind, record), protect)[0]
                if entry is None and kind == "container":
                    entry = docker.mount_protected(engine, record, protect)[0]
                if entry is not None:
                    # The protect list comes first; what could not be read of it is treated as covered.
                    answer["refused"][name] = "unread" if entry == "unread" else "space_protected:" + str(entry)
                    continue
                if any(held.get("kind") == kind and held.get("state") not in SPACE_ENDED
                       and (held.get("locator") or {}).get("engine") == engine
                       and (held.get("locator") or {}).get("name") == locator["name"]
                       for held in ledger.items().values()):
                    answer["held"].append(name)
                    continue
                made = runner.declare(kind, locator, given["owner"], "census", pool=pool)
                if made.get("ok"):
                    made = dict(runner.confirm(made["item"]), item=made["item"])
                if not made.get("ok"):
                    answer["refused"][name] = str(made.get("reason"))
                    continue
                answer["declared"][made["item"]] = locator
    return answer


def _space_finish(space, given, environ=None):
    """The run's end, in this order (design 6.9 and 8.1): the census of the run's worktree and of
    its Docker objects, run finished through Signal, the release of the hold. Each is written before
    this returns. It is made only when the command's exit is known and the kernel shows nothing of
    the run now: its supervisor has ended, its containment group was read and is empty, and no
    process in the table carries its label. Otherwise nothing is made and the reason says what held
    it. Rule 3 is answered for this run by the same read, made again each time a rule asks, and the
    hold is released only if the read still finds nothing after Signal. Returns {sent, reason,
    holders, census, docker, signal, items, hold}."""
    sp, ledger, run = _space_module("control_space"), space["ledger"], given["run"]
    runner = _space_runner(space)
    end = {"sent": False, "reason": None, "holders": [], "census": None, "docker": None, "signal": None,
           "items": {}, "hold": None}

    def held():
        """Why the run is not over now, or None."""
        if not isinstance(given.get("status"), int):
            return "not_conclusive", []
        if given.get("unread"):
            return "group_unread", []  # the supervisor ended with no word, or could not read the run
        seen = _space_judged(space, given, environ)
        if seen["group"] == "unread":
            return "group_unread", []
        holders = seen["holders"]
        if any(one["how"] in ("descendant", "group") for one in holders) or seen["group"] != "empty":
            return "group_not_empty", holders
        if any(one["how"] == "label" for one in holders):
            return "label_carried", holders  # a process outside the command's tree carries the run's label
        if any(one["how"] == "unread" for one in holders):
            return "label_unread", holders
        if holders:
            return "group_not_empty", holders  # the supervisor is still there: it has not seen the end
        return None
    found = held()
    if found is not None:
        # What is left of the run may still write: nothing of it is judged, and its hold stays held.
        end["reason"], end["holders"] = found
        return end
    if given.get("worktree"):
        # The census before Signal: the build output the command left is declared now, so run
        # finished can reach it; what is work gets no item and keeps its worktree.
        try:
            end["census"] = _space_module("control_launch").space_census(
                dict(space, ledger=runner), given["worktree"], "run_finished", owner=given.get("owner"),
                also=[path for path in (given.get("scratch"),) if path])
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - a census that could not run records nothing
            end["census"] = {"refusal": "census_failed", "said": str(error)[:300]}
    # The Docker part of the census, before Signal: the run's labelled objects are items now.
    try:
        end["docker"] = _space_docker(space, runner, given)
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - objects that could not be read are left as they are
        end["docker"] = {"refusal": "docker_census_failed", "said": str(error)[:300]}
    try:
        subject = {"run": run}
        # The Ledger's own rule, asked before anything is sent: space-runner signals only run
        # finished, and only for exactly the run the authority issued to it.
        ledger.permit(sp.RUNNER, sp.SIGNAL, {"event": "run_finished", "subject": subject})
        wired = {name: space[name] for name in SPACE_SIGNAL_GIVEN if name in space}
        others = wired.get("owner_live")
        # Rule 3 for this run is a read of the kernel, made when the rule asks: never a kept answer.
        wired["owner_live"] = lambda owner: (
            {"conclusive": True, "group_empty": held() is None}
            if ((owner or {}).get("run") or {}).get("id") == run else others(owner) if others else None)
        answer = _space_module("control_space_signal").signal(
            runner, "run_finished", subject, principal=sp.RUNNER, emitter=SPACE_EMITTER, **wired)
        end["sent"] = answer.get("ok") is True
        end["signal"] = {name: answer.get(name) for name in ("ok", "reason", "items", "carried", "left",
                                                               "outcomes", "counts")}
        if not end["sent"]:
            end["reason"] = str(answer.get("reason"))
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - what could not be signalled is not removed
        end["reason"] = "%s: %s" % (type(error).__name__, str(error)[:300])
    try:
        end["items"] = {identity: record.get("state") for identity, record in sorted(ledger.items().items())
                        if ((record.get("owner") or {}).get("run") or {}).get("id") == run}
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        pass
    if given.get("hold"):
        # The release is last: after Signal (design 6.7), and only for a run that is still over.
        try:
            again = held()
            if again is not None:
                end["hold"] = "held:" + again[0]
            else:
                done = runner.release(given["hold"])
                end["hold"] = "released" if done.get("ok") else str(done.get("reason"))
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - a hold that could not be released is named
            end["hold"] = "%s: %s" % (type(error).__name__, str(error)[:300])
    return end


def _space_ended(space, given, environ=None):
    """The run's end for a run whose command left something: sleep until the kernel shows nothing
    of it that can be waited for (_space_wait: the exit of each holder wakes it), then the end
    (_space_finish). It kills nothing and has no time limit."""
    _space_wait(lambda: _space_judged(space, given, environ))
    return _space_finish(space, given, environ)


def space_run(rest, space=None, why=None, environ=None, cwd=None, stderr=None, report=None):
    """veldo space run [--station build|review|gate] [--label L] -- COMMAND ...

    Asks admission, declares the run, starts the command in its own containment group and returns
    the command's own exit status as soon as the command exits. When the kernel then shows nothing
    of the run, the census, run finished and the release are made before it returns. When the
    command left a process, they are not: the processes are named, the run's items stay held by the
    run's supervisor, and the end follows their exit. In act mode with too little space it prints
    the numbers, does not start the command and returns 75; in report mode it prints the numbers and
    starts it. With no ledger it says why and runs the command unchanged. `report`, when given, is
    filled with what was done."""
    stderr = stderr or sys.stderr
    environ = dict(os.environ if environ is None else environ)
    where = os.getcwd() if cwd is None else str(cwd)
    report = {} if report is None else report
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="veldo space run")
    parser.add_argument("--station", choices=SPACE_STATIONS, default="build")
    parser.add_argument("--label")
    parser.add_argument("command", nargs=argparse.REMAINDER)
    args = parser.parse_args(list(rest))
    command = args.command[1:] if args.command[:1] == ["--"] else args.command
    if not command:
        parser.error("a command to run, after --")
    space, why = _space_usable(space, why, environ)
    if space is None:
        stderr.write("veldo space run: %s: the command runs unchanged\n" % why)
        report.update(wrapped=False, why=why)
        return _space_plain(command, environ, where)
    supervisor = _SpaceSupervisor(command, environ, where)
    try:
        given = _space_begin(space, args.station, args.label, where, start=supervisor.start)
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 - a wrapper never refuses the command it rides on
        stderr.write("veldo space run: the run is not recorded (%s): the command runs unchanged\n"
                     % (str(error)[:300],))
        report.update(wrapped=False, why=str(error)[:300])
        return _space_plain(command, environ, where)
    report.update(wrapped=True, run=given["run"], scope=given["scope"], station=given["station"],
                  hold=given["hold"], answer=given["answer"], reason=given["reason"], scratch=given["scratch"],
                  worktree=given["worktree"], environment=dict(given["environment"]), notes=given["notes"],
                  given=given)
    for note in given["notes"]:
        stderr.write("veldo space run: %s\n" % note)
    if given["answer"] == "waiting":
        # Act mode with too little space: the numbers, exit 75, and the command is not started.
        # Nothing is queued: the hold is released, and the next start is a new hold (design 6.7).
        stderr.write("veldo space run: NOT STARTED, waiting for space: %s\n" % _space_numbers(given["reason"]))
        try:
            done = _space_runner(space).release(given["hold"])
            report["released"] = done.get("ok") is True
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            report["released"] = False
        report.update(started=False, status=SPACE_WAIT)
        return SPACE_WAIT
    if given["answer"] == "advised":
        stderr.write("veldo space run: would wait for space (report mode, the command starts): %s\n"
                     % _space_numbers(given["reason"]))
    if given["supervisor"] is None:
        # No supervisor could be started: the command still runs, and nobody can say its run is over.
        supervisor.drop()
        status, holders = _space_plain(command, dict(environ, **given["environment"]), where), None
    else:
        supervisor.go(given, _space_group(space, environ))
        status, holders, given["group"] = supervisor.result()
    given["status"], given["unread"] = status, holders is None
    report.update(started=True, status=status, holders=holders, group=given["group"],
                  staying=supervisor.staying)
    end = _space_finish(space, given, environ)
    report["end"] = end
    if not end["sent"]:
        stderr.write("veldo space run: run finished was not sent (%s): nothing of the run was judged%s\n"
                     % (end["reason"], "; it follows the exit of process " + ", ".join(
                         str(one["pid"]) for one in end["holders"]) if end["holders"] else ""))
    return status


def _space_refused(verb, stderr, reason):
    stderr.write("veldo space %s: refused %s\n" % (verb, reason))
    return 1


def space_record(rest, space=None, why=None, environ=None, stdout=None, stderr=None):
    """veldo space record --kind K --path P [--ticket T], and then veldo space record --confirm ITEM:
    a host script reports a worktree or an agent repository it makes, around the creation (design
    6.2): the first form before, the second after. The item is declared as space-observer, with the
    creator record and the scope the path is inside as its owner, and confirmed once the thing is
    there.

    NOTHING THAT EXISTS IS EVER RECORDED BY THIS VERB, AND NOTHING IS EVER RECORDED AS SCRATCH.
    Scratch is not work by its declaration, so an item of that kind is removed unsaved when its
    owner ends: only what creates the scratch may declare it, at creation (the wrapper for a run,
    the session hook for a session). Here the kind is a worktree or an agent repository, which
    every rule and the keep step protect, and the path must not exist: what is already on disk is
    reconcile's to adopt, on the owner's word.

    Refused by name, with nothing written: an unknown kind (space_unknown_kind:<kind>), a kind this
    verb does not record (space_record_kind:<kind>), a path outside every known root
    (space_outside_roots), a path on the protect list (space_protected:<entry>), a path that exists
    (space_record_exists), a path inside a recorded worktree or agent repository
    (space_record_inside:<item>), a session as the owner (space_record_session), an item this verb
    did not declare or with nothing at its path (space_record_not_declared, space_record_absent),
    and whatever else the Ledger refuses. Exit 0 with the item on standard output, 1 for a refusal;
    with no ledger it says why and exits 0."""
    import json
    stdout, stderr = stdout or sys.stdout, stderr or sys.stderr
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="veldo space record")
    parser.add_argument("--kind")
    parser.add_argument("--path")
    parser.add_argument("--ticket")
    parser.add_argument("--session")
    parser.add_argument("--confirm")
    args = parser.parse_args(list(rest))
    if (args.confirm is None) == (args.kind is None) or (args.kind is None) != (args.path is None):
        parser.error("--kind K --path P, or --confirm ITEM")
    space, why = _space_usable(space, why, environ)
    if space is None:
        stderr.write("veldo space record: %s: nothing is recorded\n" % why)
        return 0
    sp, ledger = _space_module("control_space"), space["ledger"]
    observer = _SpaceAs(ledger, sp, sp.OBSERVER)
    if args.confirm is not None:
        record = ledger.item(args.confirm) or {}
        if (record.get("creator") != "record" or record.get("state") != "declared"
                or record.get("kind") not in SPACE_RECORD_KINDS):
            return _space_refused("record", stderr, "space_record_not_declared")
        if not os.path.lexists(record["locator"]["path"]):
            return _space_refused("record", stderr, "space_record_absent")
        confirmed = observer.confirm(args.confirm)
        if not confirmed.get("ok"):
            return _space_refused("record", stderr, str(confirmed.get("reason")))
        stdout.write(json.dumps({"item": args.confirm, "state": confirmed.get("state")}, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
        return 0
    if args.kind not in sp.KINDS:
        return _space_refused("record", stderr, "space_unknown_kind:" + args.kind)
    if args.kind not in SPACE_RECORD_KINDS:
        # Never scratch: no verb relabels a path as something that is removed unsaved.
        return _space_refused("record", stderr, "space_record_kind:" + args.kind)
    if args.session:
        # What a session owns is its scratch, and that is the session hook's to declare.
        return _space_refused("record", stderr, "space_record_session")
    refusal = _space_path_refusal(ledger, sp, args.path, args.kind)
    if refusal is not None:
        return _space_refused("record", stderr, refusal)
    path = sp.canonical_path(args.path)
    if os.path.lexists(path):
        # Declare comes before create (design 6.2). What is there already was not made by the caller.
        return _space_refused("record", stderr, "space_record_exists")
    inside = _space_worktree(ledger, path)
    if inside is not None:
        return _space_refused("record", stderr, "space_record_inside:" + inside[0])
    owner = {"scope": _space_scope(ledger, sp, path)}
    if args.ticket:
        owner["ticket"] = args.ticket
    made = observer.declare(args.kind, {"path": path}, owner, "record")
    if not made.get("ok"):
        return _space_refused("record", stderr, str(made.get("reason")))
    stdout.write(json.dumps({"item": made["item"], "state": "declared"}, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    return 0


def space_event(rest, space=None, why=None, environ=None, stdout=None, stderr=None):
    """veldo space event --type T [--path P ...] [--session S] [--repository R --ref N --new C]: a
    host script reports what happened or what it gave up. The types are ref_changed, session_started,
    session_ended and released. The event is handed to the Ledger's event command as space-observer:
    it leaves one event record and returns, and removes nothing (design 6.9). Refused by name, with
    nothing sent: an unknown type (space_unknown_event:<type>), and for every path it names a path
    outside every known root (space_outside_roots) or on the protect list (space_protected:<entry>).
    A Ledger that has no event command yet answers space_no_command:space_event: the event is then
    retained in the spool, nothing becomes due, and the verb says so. Exit 0 with the Ledger's answer
    on standard output, 1 for a refusal. With no ledger the notification is spooled and the verb exits
    0. The state root is the option, VELDO_SPACE_STATE_ROOT, the wired ledger's root, or the user's
    XDG state folder under veldo. Session fields include working-folder, pid and started (kernel ticks);
    defaults describe the caller. Released records carry creator, default record, for the consumer."""
    import json
    import socket
    stdout, stderr = stdout or sys.stdout, stderr or sys.stderr
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="veldo space event")
    parser.add_argument("--type", required=True, dest="kind")
    parser.add_argument("--path", action="append", default=[])
    parser.add_argument("--session")
    parser.add_argument("--repository")
    parser.add_argument("--ref")
    parser.add_argument("--new")
    parser.add_argument("--state-root")
    parser.add_argument("--working-folder")
    parser.add_argument("--pid", type=int)
    parser.add_argument("--started", type=int)
    parser.add_argument("--creator", default="record")
    args = parser.parse_args(list(rest))
    env = os.environ if environ is None else environ
    spool = _space_module("control_space_spool")

    def notification(subject=None):
        if args.kind in spool.SESSION_TYPES:
            process = _space_process(args.pid or os.getppid())
            if args.started is None and process is None:
                raise ValueError("the session process could not be read")
            one = spool.session_entry(args.kind, args.session, args.working_folder or os.getcwd(),
                                      args.pid or os.getppid(), args.started or process["started"],
                                      socket.gethostname() or "unknown")
        elif args.kind == "released":
            one = spool.entry(args.kind, {"paths": list(args.path), "creator": args.creator},
                              "event", socket.gethostname() or "unknown")
        else:
            one = spool.entry(args.kind, subject or {"repository": args.repository, "ref": args.ref,
                                                    "old": None, "new": args.new},
                              "reference-transaction", socket.gethostname() or "unknown")
        return one

    def pending_event(subject=None):
        # The socket and its consumer are separate tickets. Preserve the notification now.
        state_root = args.state_root or env.get("VELDO_SPACE_STATE_ROOT")
        if not state_root and space is not None:
            state_root = space["ledger"].state_root
        if not state_root:
            base = env.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or os.path.join(env.get("HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~"),
                                                            ".local", "state")
            state_root = os.path.join(base, "veldo")
        return spool.submit(notification(subject), state_root, installation=env.get("VELDO_INSTALLATION"))
    space, why = _space_usable(space, why, environ)
    if space is None:
        try:
            pending_event()
            stderr.write("veldo space event: %s: event spooled\n" % why)
        except Exception as error:
            stderr.write("veldo space event: %s: could not spool (%s)\n" % (why, error))
        return 0
    sp, ledger = _space_module("control_space"), space["ledger"]
    if args.kind not in SPACE_EVENT_TYPES:
        return _space_refused("event", stderr, "space_unknown_event:" + args.kind)
    for path in args.path:
        refusal = _space_path_refusal(ledger, sp, path)
        if refusal is not None:
            return _space_refused("event", stderr, refusal)
    subject = {}
    if args.kind == "ref_changed":
        if not (args.repository and args.ref and args.new) or args.path or args.session:
            return _space_refused("event", stderr, "invalid_input:ref_changed names a repository, a ref and "
                                                   "the new commit")
        try:
            repository = sp.canonical_locator("branch", {"repository": args.repository, "ref": args.ref})
        except sp.Refused as refusal:
            return _space_refused("event", stderr, refusal.code)
        resolved = dict(repository, repository=sp.resolved_path(repository["repository"]))
        entry = (sp.protected_by("branch", repository, ledger.protect)
                 or sp.protected_by("branch", resolved, ledger.protect, sp.resolved_path))
        if entry is not None:
            return _space_refused("event", stderr, "space_protected:" + entry)
        if not sp.inside_roots("branch", resolved, ledger.resolved_roots, ledger.engines):
            return _space_refused("event", stderr, "space_outside_roots")
        subject = {"repository": repository["repository"], "ref": args.ref, "old": None, "new": args.new}
    elif args.kind == "released":
        if not args.path or args.session or args.repository or args.ref or args.new:
            return _space_refused("event", stderr, "invalid_input:released names the paths given up")
        subject = {"paths": [sp.canonical_path(path) for path in args.path]}
    else:
        if not args.session or args.repository or args.ref or args.new:
            return _space_refused("event", stderr, "invalid_input:a session event names its session")
        subject = {"session": args.session, "paths": [sp.canonical_path(path) for path in args.path]}
    try:
        subject = notification(subject)["subject"]
    except (spool.Malformed, ValueError) as error:
        return _space_refused("event", stderr, "invalid_input:" + str(error))
    try:
        answer = _SpaceAs(ledger, sp, sp.OBSERVER).event(
            args.kind, subject, {"verb": "event", "host": socket.gethostname() or "unknown"})
    except Exception as error:
        try:
            pending_event(subject)
            stderr.write("veldo space event: event spooled (%s)\n" % error)
        except Exception as failure:
            stderr.write("veldo space event: could not spool (%s)\n" % failure)
        return 0
    if not answer.get("ok"):
        reason = str(answer.get("reason"))
        if reason.startswith("space_no_command:"):
            try:
                pending_event(subject)
            except Exception as error:
                stderr.write("veldo space event: could not spool (%s)\n" % error)
            stderr.write("veldo space event: not recorded (%s): nothing becomes due and nothing is removed\n"
                         % reason)
            stdout.write(json.dumps({"recorded": False, "reason": reason, "type": args.kind}, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
            return 0
        return _space_refused("event", stderr, reason)
    stdout.write(json.dumps({"recorded": True, "type": args.kind, "event": answer.get("event")},
                            sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    return 0


def route(argv):
    """Dispatch one argv to the right module and return an exit code. All fail-loud
    paths land here: an unknown subcommand, bad arguments, an unknown account, a
    refused build, or a refused spawn each become a nonzero exit with an honest
    message, never a silent success."""
    parser = build_parser()
    try:
        ns = parser.parse_args(argv)
    except SystemExit as ex:
        return ex.code if isinstance(ex.code, int) else 2

    cmd = ns.subcommand
    rest = ns.rest

    try:
        if cmd is None:
            parser.error("a subcommand is required (one of: %s)" % ", ".join(KNOWN_SUBCOMMANDS))
        if cmd in ("status", "watch"):
            # forward verbatim to runstatus.py's own CLI (its subcommand IS the name)
            return _runstatus()._cli([cmd] + rest)
        if cmd == "account":
            # forward verbatim to accounts.py's own add/list CLI
            return _accounts().main(rest)
        if cmd in ("answer", "steer", "abort"):
            # forward verbatim to runcmd.py's own answer/steer/abort CLI
            return _runcmd().main([cmd] + rest)
        if cmd == "supervisor":
            # forward verbatim to supervisor.py's own install/status/uninstall CLI (the opt-in,
            # off-by-default external resume timer). It fails loud on a missing schedule or
            # subcommand and NEVER spawns a session; nothing runs unless the user runs it.
            return _supervisor().main(rest)
        if cmd == "channel":
            # forward verbatim to the activation module's own command surface (VELDO-0138): the owner's
            # signed qualify, activate and stop, and his delegate (VELDO-0140), applied by the running
            # authority service.
            return _channel().main(rest)
        if cmd == "memory":
            return _mod("veldo_cli_memory", ".veldo/control_factory_setup_memory.py").main(rest)
        if cmd == "space":
            # record, event and replay are what the git hooks run (VELDO-0311): one entry for what git
            # just did, handed to the authority or left in the spool. They never fail the git command.
            # VELDO-0313: the run wrapper, and the record and event verbs of a host script (the forms
            # that name a kind or an event type; the forms the git hooks run name neither).
            if rest and rest[0] == "run":
                return space_run(rest[1:])
            if rest and rest[0] == "supervise":
                return space_supervise(rest[1:])
            if rest and rest[0] == "contained":
                return space_contained(rest[1:])
            if rest and rest[0] == "record" and (_space_names(rest, "--kind") or _space_names(rest, "--confirm")):
                return space_record(rest[1:])
            if rest and rest[0] == "event" and _space_names(rest, "--type"):
                return space_event(rest[1:])
            if rest and rest[0] in ("record", "event", "replay"):
                # With no central installation each verb says so (VELDO-0313 AC4); what the hooks get
                # back is unchanged: record and event exit 0 whatever happens.
                if not _space_names(rest, "--installation") and _space_wired()[1] == SPACE_NO_INSTALLATION:
                    sys.stderr.write("veldo space %s: %s\n" % (rest[0], SPACE_NO_INSTALLATION))
                return _mod("veldo_cli_space_spool", ".veldo/control_space_spool.py").main(rest)
            # forward verbatim to the space ledger's own command surface (VELDO-0326): a hook or a helper
            # signs as its host principal with that principal's key, never the owner's.
            return _mod("veldo_cli_space", ".veldo/control_space.py").main(rest)
        if cmd == "factory":
            # forward verbatim to the setup module's own command surface (VELDO-0139): the owner's
            # one setup of a real factory on this host, ordering and checking the existing pieces, and
            # (VELDO-0171) his passkey command, which signs the one registration he names.
            return _factory().main(rest)
        if cmd == "mirror":
            # the opt-in, off-by-default live mirror runner (PLAN-0010 W5): one reconcile pass that
            # applies the one-way mirror (status, comments, artifact links, the ready-to-test
            # reassignment) to the tracker. It creates NO timer, daemon, or auto-start and spawns
            # NOTHING; a cadence is the operator's own poll interval when they run it again. The
            # runner is REPO-ONLY build machinery (like the other tracker_*.py modules), so a pack
            # that did not lay it fails loud and honestly here rather than import a missing module.
            if not (ROOT / ".veldo" / "tracker_mirror_runner.py").exists():
                sys.stderr.write("veldo mirror: the tracker mirror runner is repo-only and is not "
                                 "present here; wire the tracker integration to use it\n")
                return 2
            return _mirror()._cli(rest)
        if cmd == "jira":
            # `veldo jira init` (WARP-0612): bootstrap a company-managed Jira project into the live
            # board - detect the project type (fail loud on team-managed), provision the lifecycle
            # status set + workflow idempotently, and mirror every plan and spec onto it, in ONE
            # pass. Like `veldo mirror` it creates NO timer, daemon, or auto-start and spawns NOTHING;
            # a re-run reconciles. The bootstrap is REPO-ONLY build machinery (like the other
            # tracker_*.py modules), so a pack that did not lay it fails loud and honestly here
            # rather than import a missing module.
            if not (ROOT / ".veldo" / "tracker_jira_init.py").exists():
                sys.stderr.write("veldo jira: the Jira bootstrap is repo-only and is not present "
                                 "here; wire the tracker integration to use it\n")
                return 2
            return _jira().main(rest)
        if cmd == "work":
            args = _work_args(rest)
            return cmd_work(account=args.account)
        if cmd == "fleet":
            args = _fleet_args(rest)
            return cmd_fleet(args.n, account=args.account)
        # unknown subcommand: fail loud, never a silent no-op
        parser.error("unknown subcommand %r (one of: %s)"
                     % (cmd, ", ".join(KNOWN_SUBCOMMANDS)))
    except SystemExit as ex:
        # argparse inside a work/fleet sub-parse (bad args) exits nonzero; honor it.
        return ex.code if isinstance(ex.code, int) else 2
    except _account_error_bases() as ex:
        sys.stderr.write("account error: %s\n" % ex)
        return 2
    except _fleet().SpawnPrimitiveError as ex:
        sys.stderr.write("fleet cannot start: %s\n" % ex)
        return 2
    except (_fleet().NoAccountAvailableError, ValueError) as ex:
        # an unpaced fleet (no token budget) or an empty account pool: honest nonzero,
        # never a traceback and never a fabricated budget.
        sys.stderr.write("fleet error: %s\n" % ex)
        return 2
    except _dispatch().EX.ExecutorError as ex:
        sys.stderr.write("build refused: %s\n" % ex)
        return 2
    except (_work().EL.Stopped, _work().CL.ClaimStopped) as ex:
        # VELDO-0052 / VELDO-0031: a named stop (no host trust, an unverified binding, no claim
        # authority wired) is said by name and is a nonzero exit, never a traceback.
        sys.stderr.write("veldo %s stopped: %s\n" % (cmd, ex.reason))
        return 2


def main(argv=None):
    return route(list(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
